<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ivan Asen II</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1241">1241</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ivan Asen II, son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-asen-i">Ivan Asen I</a>, was tsar of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> from 1218 to 1241. In 1230 he defeated and captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodore-komnenos-doukas">Theodore Komnenos Doukas</a>, ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epirus">Epirus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaloniki">Thessalonica</a>, at Klokotnitsa, after which Bulgaria stretched from the Adriatic to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> and Thessalonica's new ruler Manuel became his client. He absorbed lands such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/melnik">Melnik</a> and in 1235 restored the Bulgarian patriarchate with Nicaean agreement.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ivan-asen-ii.jpg" alt="Ivan Asen II" width="320" height="272" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CNG Coins · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coin_of_Ivan_Asen_II.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ivan Asen II was born in the 1190s, the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-asen-i">Ivan Asen I</a> and Elena. His father was killed in 1196 and his uncle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaloyan-of-bulgaria">Kaloyan</a> succeeded. After Kaloyan was murdered in 1207, Ivan Asen had a strong claim, but Kaloyan's Cuman widow married Boril, the son of one of Kaloyan's sisters, who was proclaimed emperor. According to George Akropolites, Ivan Asen fled to the lands of the Russians. He returned with hired soldiers, probably after Boril's ally <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andrew-ii-of-hungary">Andrew II of Hungary</a> left for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fifth-crusade">Fifth Crusade</a> in 1217, besieged Boril in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/veliko-tarnovo">Tarnovo</a>, and captured and blinded him in 1218.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andrew-ii-of-hungary">Andrew II</a>, returning from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fifth-crusade">Fifth Crusade</a> in late 1218, was not allowed to cross <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> until he promised his daughter Maria to Ivan Asen. Early in 1230 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodore-komnenos-doukas">Theodore Komnenos Doukas</a> invaded along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maritsa">Maritsa</a>, and the armies met at Klokotnitsa in March or April. Ivan Asen marched into battle holding a copy of his peace treaty with Theodore high in the air, a reference to Theodore's betrayal. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgarians">Bulgarians</a> captured Theodore and his principal officials; Ivan Asen released the common soldiers and later, after a plot, had Theodore blinded. His troops took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ohrid">Ohrid</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prilep">Prilep</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serres">Serres</a>, Adrianople and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plovdiv">Plovdiv</a>, and his son-in-law Manuel Doukas took control of the Empire of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaloniki">Thessaloniki</a>.</p><p>Control of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/via-egnatia">Via Egnatia</a>, the trade route between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaloniki">Thessaloniki</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durres">Durazzo</a>, brought revenue; he struck gold coins at a new mint in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ohrid">Ohrid</a> and built in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/veliko-tarnovo">Tarnovo</a>, including the Church of the Holy Forty Martyrs, which commemorated Klokotnitsa. In 1235 he met <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-iii-doukas-vatatzes">John III Vatatzes</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicaea">Nicaea</a> at Lampsacus, where Patriarch Germanus II recognised Joachim I as patriarch of the Bulgarian Church, acknowledging its autocephaly. The allies besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> unsuccessfully in 1235 and again the next year. In 1237 Ivan Asen broke the alliance, fearing that Vatatzes would gain most from the fall of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-empire">Latin Empire</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1237 Mongol invasions drove thousands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cumans">Cumans</a> across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>. Ivan Asen later married Irene, who had been captured with her father Theodore in 1230. The date of his death is unknown; Alexandru Madgearu writes that he most probably died in May or June 1241. Historian Jean W. Sedlar described him as the last really powerful ruler of Bulgaria. His reign ended during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongol-invasion-of-europe">Mongol invasion of Europe</a>; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> invaded Bulgaria in 1242 and forced it to pay yearly tribute, and his successor's minority gave rise to boyar factions. His seal is shown on the reverse of the Bulgarian 2 lev banknote.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ivan Asen II” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Ports such as Varna, Mesembria (Nesebar) and Anchialos linked the country to wider trade, and <b>Ivan Asen II</b> granted merchants from Ragusa (Dubrovnik) a charter allowing them to trade freely in his lands; Venetians and Genoese became prominent in the fourteenth century, when the tsars issued silver coinage…</blockquote><a href="/empire/second-bulgarian-empire">Second Bulgarian Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Tsar <b>Ivan Asen II</b> of Bulgaria routed and captured Theodore Komnenos Doukas, ruler of Epirus and Thessalonica, who had broken a treaty and invaded Bulgaria.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/march-9">9 March in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The Bulgarian tsar <b>Ivan Asen II</b> absorbed his lands around 1230, after defeating Epirus at Klokotnitsa, into the Second Bulgarian Empire.</blockquote><a href="/state/despotate-of-melnik">Despotate of Melnik</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1230 he attacked Bulgaria but was defeated and captured by <b>Ivan Asen II</b> at Klokotnitsa and later blinded.</blockquote><a href="/state/empire-of-thessalonica">Empire of Thessalonica</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/veliko-tarnovo">Veliko Tarnovo</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=43.08,25.63&amp;year=1231" aria-label="Veliko Tarnovo on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of Ivan Asen II"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4727 3886h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4727 3886h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=43.08,25.63&amp;year=1231">What was here in 1231? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
